      "content": "If I had to rank them, I'd say crime rates are the most reliable indicator of a neighborhood's decline. It's a tangible, quantifiable metric that shows a community's willingness to engage in lawlessness. But if you want to get into the nitty-gritty, high levels of poverty and unemployment are also strong predictors of economic instability. And let's not forget about the role of government dependency - areas with high rates of welfare recipients and other forms of government-subsidized support tend to be less economically resilient. But honestly, if you're looking for a single metric, crime rates are the way to go.",
